//BUG="a method that calls itself is instantiated again on every recursive call"
//OUT="6"

// A function may call itself because `normalize_function`
// (src/compiler/normalizer/mod.rs) first looks the call up in the instance
// cache: if an instance with the same argument types is already being built,
// the call reuses it. The lookup, `check_instance_cache`, compares the
// argument types with `TypeResolver::are_equal`, which only says yes when
// two type labels have already been unified.
//
// For a method call that never holds. The lowerer wraps the receiver in an
// `AutoRef` (src/compiler/lowerer/mod.rs, the `MethodCall` arm), so the type
// of the first argument is a fresh label whose reference depth is still
// open. `find_matching_function` does unify it with the candidate's
// `self: &Counter`, but only to test the candidate, and rolls that back. The
// argument is tied to the parameter for good a few lines *after* the cache
// has been consulted. So the recursive call is a miss, a new instance is
// started, its body contains the same call, and the compile ends with
//
//     This function is in the 100-th recursive call in the normalization phase.
//
// The return hint here rules out the documented cause of that message
// (05_functions/40 and 41): the return type is known from the signature.
// The identical function written outside the struct, taking `c: &Counter`,
// compiles and prints 6. Two methods that call each other in a cycle fail
// like this one, and so does every recursive method on a templated struct.
//
// That takes away the recursive `find` of a union-find, a segment tree's
// `query`, a depth-first search on a graph struct — the methods this
// language's niche is made of.
//
// The fix is to bind the arguments to the chosen signature before the cache
// is looked at, so that the receiver has its final type when it is compared.

struct Counter
    step: i32

    fn down n: i32 -> i32
        if n == 0
            ret 0
        ret self.step + self.down(n - 1)

fn main
    c = Counter step 2
    out "{c.down(3)}"
